  "grafo_evidencia": {
    "nodes": [
      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "44bd9f39-0696-5ef4-8028-a10b78916c01",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Alexander Borodin\n- Works: Stranger in Paradise, Polovtsian Dances, Prince Igor, Gliding Dance of the Maidens\n- Concepts: Polovtsian, Polovetsian, Kipchaks, Cumans, Rus' people\n\nFacts:\n- Alexander Borodin composed the opera Prince Igor in 1890.\n- The Polovtsian Dances, also called the Polovetsian Dances, form an exotic scene at the end of Act II of Alexander Borodin's opera Prince Igor.\n- The Russian title Половецкие пляски is transliterated as \"Polovetskie plyaski\" for the Polovtsian Dances.\n- The Russian term \"Polovtsy\" refers to the Kipchaks and Cumans in the naming used by the Rus' people.\n- The melody of the song Stranger in Paradise was taken from music by Alexander Borodin.\n- In Stranger in Paradise, the borrowed melody specifically comes from \"Gliding Dance of the Maidens\" from the Polovtsian Dances in Prince Igor.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "6658df64-b091-5329-b7b9-2a6f1f0504de",
        "text": "Page: Stranger in Paradise (song). Sentence: Like almost all the music in that show, the melody was taken from music composed by Alexander Borodin (1833–1887), in this case, the \"Gliding Dance of the Maidens\", from the \"Polovtsian Dances\" in the opera \"Prince Igor\" (1890).\nPage: Polovtsian Dances. Sentence: The Polovtsian Dances, or Polovetsian Dances (Russian: Половецкие пляски, \"Polovetskie plyaski\" from the Russian \"Polovtsy\"—the name given to the Kipchaks and Cumans by the Rus' people) form an exotic scene at the end of Act II of Alexander Borodin's opera \"Prince Igor\".",
